{"authored" [{"question" "q-86861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/534f6ef0206bd4281320968c8a60b88f7f237e48c31e3d10c1080ddb69b9f56f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,1]$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/review.json" "file_sha256" "f950d69e3a3c19e6aa06adc8a25f4119e2d184f40f7f983dee1583e392501379" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-86861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/review.json" "file_sha256" "f950d69e3a3c19e6aa06adc8a25f4119e2d184f40f7f983dee1583e392501379" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-86861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/review.json" "file_sha256" "f950d69e3a3c19e6aa06adc8a25f4119e2d184f40f7f983dee1583e392501379" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-86861/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-86861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/534f6ef0206bd4281320968c8a60b88f7f237e48c31e3d10c1080ddb69b9f56f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\